  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Wetting rain is expected areawide Tuesday night\nand Wednesday, with a chance of storms. Storms are not expected\nto be severe Tuesday night but may become strong to possibly \nsevere Wednesday afternoon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWarm front will lift through Tuesday night with a 40-50kt low-\nlevel jet bringing in a slug of moisture. In fact PW values may \ntop 2.00 inches over Downeast which is 2-3 SD above normal and \nfalls into the 99th percentile. Plenty of elevated instability \nexists to bring isolated thunder to srn areas on Tuesday night. \n\nThis first system of locally heavy rain looks to bring around 1 inch \nto Downeast areas with in strong synoptic lift as they will likely \nbe in the favorable quad of the upr jet overnight. \n\nRain moves east into New Brunswick around 12z Wednesday with a \npossible break during the morning hours before convection develops \nahead of a pre-frontal trof. Although there will be little in the \nway of sunshine guidance is showing sfc-based CAPES of around 1000 \nJ/kg over the North Woods in the afternoon. Given the amount of \nshear, cannot rule out an isolated severe storm over nrn and wrn \nareas bringing damaging winds especially in areas that may see peeks \nof sunshine. Rainfall totals Wednesday afternoon across the north \nshould range from 0.25-0.50 inches. Patchy to areas of fog can be \nexpected Tuesday night into Wednesday night, more prominent along \nthe coast, in the warm and humid airmass.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Rough seas along the Downeast coast Wednesday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nSeas of 5-8 feet are expected along the shoreline on Wednesday.\nThe latest peak wave periods from NWPS at the shore are showing\naround 8 feet in the afternoon and 9 feet during the evening \nand overnight. The rip risk along the coast is high for \nWednesday and criteria for high surf advisory may be met so \neither way it will be rough for anyone along the coast and \nshould exercise caution if recreating. Situations like this have\nresulted in people being swept into the ocean by larger waves \ncrashing on the cliffs at Acadia.",
